rooms_desc ({"You are standing before a large mansion that can be none other than thehome of Elvis.  A wall surrounds the property, with a massive white gatein the front.  A figure of a musician on the gate, surrounded by musicalnotes, catches your attention.  There is a long asphalt driveway, surrounded by trees of all types, adding to its serenity, with littleanimals darting in and out.  Large pillars loom before you, adding to themagnificence of this house.  There is a huge door to the east, and you havea feeling that more surprises await inside.","You are standing in the foyer of Elvis's house.  You can't help but marvelin its splendor.  You are standing beneath the largest chandelier that youhave ever seen.  To your left lies the dining room, with a large table that is customary of mansions.  Flowers are scattered about the room.  Toyour right lies the living room, and a couch of unbelievable size catchesyour attention.  The decorations are very modest, and pictures of Elvis'sMUD friends dot the wall, to the right and left.  In the back of the roomlies the music room, which is separated from the living room by a hugestained glass entryway.  A piano is back here, and you get the feeling thatElvis spends his quiet moments here.  To the east of the foyer is a grandstaircase going up, and to the north a stairway goes down.","You ascend the grand staircase, leaving the downstairs behind.  You comefinally to the master bedroom, and you immediately notice something peculiarabout it.  The whole room, and all of its decorations, are entirely black.The walls are a deep black, and there are no windows in this room.  All ofthe lights are very dim, but you are able to discern a HUGE waterbed in thecenter of the room, made up with a black satin bedspread and sheets.  Thereis also an entertainment center in the corner of the room, along with a massive shelf of books.","You are now in Elvis's TV room.  In the back of the room is a narrowstaircase, leading to the foyer.  The walls and ceiling surrounding the stairs are mirrored, giving you a dizzy feeling.  A bar is in the corner ofthe room, able to furnish any thirst.  On the far wall are three TV's, soElvis can watch three channels at once.  Statuettes of ebony and ivory monkeys are on the floor.  The carpeting is deep shag, and it makes yourfeet disappear as you walk on it.  Stacks of CDs are in the corner, and astereo is here also.  This stereo pumps music to every room in the house.The furniture here is all white leather, including a couch that Elvis likesto nap on.  A hallway leads west, and the stairway leads to the foyer south.","You are now in a room of seemingly limitless size.  Upon entering, you didn'tthink that this room was very big, but now that you see it, it is enormous!This is the MUD room, where Elvis likes to brag of his adventures while playing Holy Mission.  Pictures on the wall depict some of his MUD memories,as well as the numerous trophies of beasts he has killed.  On one wall is a case containing the many weapons Elvis has used, now on permanent displayand not really usable.  A small gold and ivory altar is in the corner of theroom, with torches on both sides.  In the middle of the room lies an immensecrystal ball, held by a statue created in the likeness of a large dragon.  Atiny staircase leads north, and the TV room is to the east.","You hear the sound of water running.  You look around and realize that youare in the famous Jungle Room.  The running water sound comes from a livewaterfall, running down the side of the wall.  The floor, the walls, and eventhe ceiling is carpeted in green, yielding near-perfect acoustics in caseElvis wants to jam with some friends.  There are tiki torches on the wallalso, but they are not lit.  The furniture is distinctly Hawaiian, withwide-armed, Polynesian chairs and varnished wooden tables.  You feel as thoughyou are at a luau, because Hawaiian music is playing as well.  Posters from\"Blue Hawaii\" and \"King Creole\" are hanging on the wall.  You notice a brightlight where the hall continues to the east.  You can also go back to the MUD room south, or go outside to the north.","You now enter Elvis's game room.  You notice that it is a stark contrastto the bright, white hues of the TV room.  Elaborately-woven tapestrieshang on the wall, depicting medieval scenes.  The tapestries are gatheredin places, adding to the effect.  The tapestry on each wall is unique, andworthy of a closer look.  The carpet is a royal red, and like all the othercarpeting, is very soft.  A pool table is the centerpiece of this room, anda rack of pool cues hangs on the wall.  On the east wall is a line of videogames, and in keeping pace with the times, a virtual reality game is also inthe corner.  The furniture is scarce here-- only a small couch sits along the north wall.  A stained glass light fixture hangs above the pool table,completing the decor of the room.  The doorway leading west goes back tothe TV room.","You now find yourself outside in the sunshine.  You are in Elvis's pooland garden area.  A pool of modest size is in the middle, with a diving areaand sliding board at the deep end.  It is surrounded by a black iron fence,to separate it from Graceland's glorious garden.  There is a walltravelling the length of the garden.  It is said to be from Europe and canbe traced back to the 12th century.  It does look old, but extraordinarilybeautiful.  Meanwhile, in the garden hundreds of species of flowers and bushescall this place home.  A glorious scent fills the air from all the flowersand bushes.  You find this place very peaceful, almost heavenly.  A lovelyfountain tops off this scene.  You can go south back into the house, orgo east to another part of the building.","As you walk into this room, you are almost blinded by the brilliant flashof gold that meets you.  Welcome to Elvis's Hall of Gold, in which is housedmost of his gold and plantinum records.  Needless to say, the records in thisplace are over a hundred in count.  A large platinum album in the cornersignifies sales of a billion records worldwide.  This corner alsohouses Elvis's three Grammys, which were for, ironically, gospel recordingsand not rock songs.  Also in this hall are outfits that Elvis has worn forshows, jewelry, and paintings sent to Elvis by adoring fans.  Plaques from the city are also here, thanking Elvis for his generous donations atChristmas.  As you leave you see, written on the wall, Elvis's favoritequote to the critics.  It reads, \"Don't criticize what youcan't understand... you never walked a mile in that man's shoes.\"A door leads to the north, and back to the Jungle Room to the west.","You enter this room, and suddenly you realize that two atmospheres suddenly come together.  In one half of the room, which is enormous, is afull-sized gym, complete with free weights, Nautilus machines, and exercisebikes.  There are mirrors along the walls in the weight room.  In theopposite corner of this side is a jacuzzi, sauna, and a large racquetballcourt where Elvis takes all challengers.  However, on the other side of theroom is a nightclub atmosphere, with about twenty tables dotting the area.Servants are bringing out fine dishes, filled with cuisine from all overthe world.  There is a stage at the head of the room, and bands of all typesare constantly playing.  You feel as though you are in Las Vegas.  A doorleads south back to the Hall of Gold, and another door heads west.",})
rooms_short ({"Entrance to Graceland","Foyer, living room, and dining room","Upstairs.","TV room","MUD room","Jungle Room","Game Room","Pool and Garden","Hall of Gold","Gym/Dining Room",})
